    },
    {
      "text": "completeness is checked in BOTH directions: over-claim (a claimed CSR the RTL lacks - caught empirically by the sweep/probe) AND under-claim (a CSR the engine implements that is neither transported nor on a named exclusion must FAIL a scripted check, not rely on human review)",
      "passed": false,
      "evidence": "Over-claim is covered ('Batched PB csr_sweep at cold-cache capture ... faults on one over-claim and bisects to name the CSR'), but under-claim relies on '--verify mismatches when a fixture writes the CSR' - i.e. only CSRs some hand-written fixture happens to exercise. There is no scripted check that an implemented-but-untransported CSR without a named exclusion must FAIL."
    },
    {
      "text": "a per-engine CSR manifest is a deliverable, carrying EMPIRICAL reset values (from a probe of the reset hart or the baseline-capture file), not values hand-typed from the spec",
      "passed": false,
      "evidence": "No per-engine CSR manifest deliverable. The artifact groups are the --describe self-report, caps unit tests, parity script, spec-level coverage CSVs (engine-independent), verify exclusions, and ladder results. Empirical reset values appear only as the uncommitted baseline cache ('not a committed artifact, but its existence after step 3 is the proof the sweep ran clean')."
    },
    {
      "text": "per-engine write-survival test fixtures are GENERATED from the manifest, with a per-CSR safe-write policy / skip-list for dangerous control CSRs (mcache_ctl / milmb class)",
      "passed": false,
      "evidence": "No fixture generator and no safe-write policy/skip-list anywhere in the document. It relies on the existing hand-written fixtures ('e2e fixtures that exercise the CSR sections (e.g. handoff_roundtrip.S, rt_csr_survival, the F5 counter fixture) are part of the ship gate')."
    },
    {
      "text": "a registration guard cross-checks the engines the driver reports against the registered artifacts and fails loudly for an unregistered or stale-config engine",
      "passed": false,
      "evidence": "No registration guard exists or is proposed. The closest mechanism is the parity flywheel (offline deriver vs --describe), which the audit itself shows is single-engine (hardcodes premium), currently FAILING, and not wired into test_all.sh; nothing cross-checks driver-reported engines against registered artifacts or fails for an unregistered/stale-config engine."
    }
  ],
